# Save-file compatibility baseline for KeeperFX: Tux Edition
#
# READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING THE NUMBER. If CI pointed you here, the number is
# almost certainly NOT the thing that is wrong.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
# ------------
# A KeeperFX saved game is a raw memory dump of `struct Game`. When the engine
# loads one, the ONLY compatibility check it performs is:
#
#     if (hdr.len != sizeof(struct Game))   -> refuse the save
#                                              (src/game_saves.c)
#
# There is no version field, no field-by-field serialisation, no migration
# path. So `sizeof(struct Game)` IS the save format version, whether anyone
# intended it to be or not.
#
# `struct Game` is enormous and deeply nested -- it contains, among much else,
# `struct Thing things_data[THINGS_COUNT]` with THINGS_COUNT = 8192 + 4096 =
# 12288. Adding one field ANYWHERE inside ANY struct it reaches changes its
# size, and every saved game ever written by an earlier build stops loading.
# There is no warning, in review or at runtime: the player just finds their
# campaign gone.
#
# WHY THE GUARD EXISTS
# --------------------
# On 2026-08-19 an upstream merge added a single field to `struct Thing`:
#
#     + GameTurn last_turn_damaged;      // 4 bytes
#
# multiplied by 12288 things, that is 49152 bytes, and `sizeof(struct Game)`
# went 53269285 -> 53318509 between builds 5488 and 5539. Five campaigns in
# progress became unloadable. Roughly a hundred builds before that one had all
# been save-compatible, which is exactly why nobody was watching. Nothing in
# the diff looked like a save-format change, because in this codebase nothing
# ever does.
#
# HOW TO REACT WHEN THE CHECK FAILS
# ---------------------------------
# The check (packaging/ci/check-save-format.sh) measures the real size with
# packaging/ci/save_format_probe.c, built by linux.mk with the engine's own
# compiler flags, and compares it with the value below.
#
# A mismatch means: the change in your working tree WILL invalidate every
# existing saved game, for you and for every player who updates.
#
#   * If that was NOT intended -- the usual case for an upstream merge, a
#     "harmless" new field, a struct reorder -- do not touch this file. Fix the
#     change instead. A field that only matters at runtime can live outside
#     `struct Game`; if it must be per-thing, ask whether an existing padding
#     byte or an unused field can carry it.
#
#   * If it IS intended and the break is accepted, update the number below in
#     the SAME commit as the change that caused it, and say so in the commit
#     message and the changelog. The release notes then have to tell players
#     their saves will not load -- CI writes that warning automatically, off
#     this file's history.
#
# Never "just make CI green" here. Silencing this line is the one action that
# throws away other people's saved games with no trace.
#
# The value is for the x86_64 System V ABI, the only target this fork ships.
# It does not vary between GCC versions -- struct layout is ABI-fixed -- so a
# mismatch is always a source change, never a toolchain difference.

sizeof_struct_game=53350681

# 2026-08-25: upstream's custom-icon feature (#5147, #5139) added
#     short icon_idx;   // 2 bytes (+ padding)
# to `struct Event`, embedded as `struct Event event[EVENTS_COUNT]` in
# `struct Game` (EVENTS_COUNT = 200). 53318509 -> 53350681 between builds
# 5554 and this one. Accepted deliberately: the field records which custom
# icon a QUICK_OBJECTIVE/QUICK_INFORMATION event/objective marker was given,
# and needs to persist for the marker's lifetime, which can span a save/load
# -- dropping it from the save would silently revert a custom icon back to
# the default one after loading, a real (if minor) behavioural regression
# from what upstream shipped, not merely a compatibility inconvenience.
